doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21269456"
repository-code: "https://github.com/mbaffour/FigureLab"
url: "https://mbaffour.github.io/FigureLab/"
abstract: >-
  FigureLab is a single-file, browser-based application for assembling
  publication-quality scientific figures from image data (microscopy, western
  blots, histology, gels, and clinical or specimen photographs). It runs
  entirely client-side with no installation, server, or internet connection
  required, so no image data ever leaves the user's computer. Features include
  grid layout with drag-to-space gutters, per-panel image adjustments
  (brightness, contrast, gamma, levels, fluorescence LUTs, and multi-channel
  composites), two-point scale-bar calibration, an annotation system with
  undo/redo, and export to PNG, JPEG, WebP, TIFF, PDF, and SVG at
  publication DPI (up to 1200 dpi for line art). A freeform editor adds
  vector data charts (bar, line, scatter, box, violin, beeswarm, heatmap, and
  Kaplan-Meier survival curves) drawn from pasted or CSV data, flowcharts with
  anchored connectors including PRISMA/CONSORT/fishbone templates,
  base-pair-scaled gene maps and genetic circuits in SBOL Visual notation
  (linear or circular, with GenBank/FASTA import), and a recolourable
  scientific-icon library. Measurements read the retained native samples of
  16-bit and floating-point TIFF imports, reporting absolute intensity at the
  acquisition bit depth rather than the 8-bit display, and disclose per result
  when they fall back to the display. Scientific-integrity safeguards are built
  in: no p-value or statistical inference is ever computed, pixel edits are
  non-destructive overlays, and AI-generated content is disclosed. It also
  exports reproducibility scripts (R/Python, including chart code),
  CSV panel metadata, and journal-style captions.
